---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/for/fashion';
const title = 'Shopify SMS apps for fashion stores';
const desc = 'Choose SMS around size availability, drop access and returns, rather than a universal send schedule.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a href="/for" class="link">← All store types</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Shopify SMS apps for fashion stores</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl">Choose SMS around size availability, drop access and returns, rather than a universal send schedule.</p>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-10">
      <h2>Match the exact variant</h2>
      <p>Keep product, color and size identifiers on a waitlist request. A restock of another size should not release that request. Recheck availability when a queued message becomes eligible; a quiet-hours delay can outlast scarce stock. Link to the requested variant and explain that an alert does not reserve it.</p>
      <h2>Coordinate drops and early access</h2>
      <p>Separate a product waitlist from general promotional permission. Select launch recipients by expressed collection interest, then check whether they already bought the item. Early access needs an actual access mechanism and a support owner; a VIP label alone does not make a public link exclusive. Use email for the lookbook and fit detail, SMS for the promised opening.</p>
      <h2>Treat returns as a different state</h2>
      <p>An exchange request is not a request to buy the same item again. Pause recovery for the affected order while sizing support is open. After an exchange completes, use the retained variant for relevant recommendations, without inferring every future size from one purchase. Give customers a way to change their preferences.</p>
      <h2>Proposed demonstration: a size restock</h2>
      <p>Ask each shortlisted provider to demonstrate an opted-in shopper waiting for a sold-out size, another size becoming available, then the requested size returning during a blocked sending period. Expect no wrong-size alert and a fresh availability check before release. Add a purchase before release and inspect whether the queued reminder is cancelled. These are proposed checks, not test results.</p>
      <h2>Measure retained purchases</h2>
      <p>Compare drop and restock cohorts separately. Track delivered-message cost, opt-outs, full-price orders and retained revenue after returns and exchanges. Keep the return window consistent when comparing cohorts. Attribution to a text does not establish that the text caused the sale; use a suitable holdout before calling the gain incremental.</p>
      <h2>Start with a variant-restock demonstration</h2>
      <p>Klaviyo documents back-in-stock forms tied to the selected variant and SMS back-in-stock flows, making it a relevant candidate for size-specific demand. Compare Postscript, which documents Shopify SMS integration, if launch messaging is the main job. Require both candidates to show availability checks after a delayed send; the documentation alone does not settle that behavior.</p>
      <p>Sequenzy describes event-driven email campaigns and transactional email. Consider it for fit guidance and lookbooks only if purchase and exchange suppressions reach it reliably. Keeping those emails in the existing system may reduce collision risk.</p>
      <p>Browse the catalog entries for <a href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a>, <a href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a>, <a href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a>, <a href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a> and <a href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a>. Request a current quote for your destinations, message types, billable segments, carrier charges and expected peak volume. Catalog links are navigation, not certification of every claim on those pages.</p>
      <h2>Launch controls and evidence</h2>
      <p>Before enabling a flow, document the permission and message purpose, recipient location handling, cancellation events and reply owner. Demonstrate opt-out handling and quiet-hour deferral with your actual configuration. Coordinate email and SMS against the same purchase and support state. Set frequency from the promised experience and observed complaints rather than a universal weekly allowance. See the <a href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a> for further review.</p>
      <p>Primary documentation reviewed November 25, 2026: <a href="https://postscript.io/postscript-integration-with-shopify">Postscript Shopify integration</a>; <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/38767539287323">Klaviyo back-in-stock forms</a>; <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/7954040204827">Klaviyo back-in-stock SMS</a>; <a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/3175406-connect-your-shopify-store-to-omnisend">Omnisend Shopify sync</a>; <a href="https://www.sequenzy.com/about">Sequenzy product scope</a>. Provider documentation supports the stated product scope; the workflow recommendations above are editorial analysis and still need a store-specific demonstration.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
